7:## Individualized resting-state functional connectivity abnormalities unveil two major depressive disorder subtypes with contrasting abnormal patterns of abnormality
15:Modern neuroimaging research has recognized that major depressive disorder (MDD) is a connectome disorder, characterized by altered functional connectivity across large-scale brain networks. However, the clinical heterogeneity, likely stemming from diverse neurobiological disturbances, complicates /uniFB01 ndings from standard group comparison methods. This variability has driven the search for MDD subtypes using objective neuroimaging markers. In this study, we sought to identify potential MDD subtypes from subjectlevel abnormalities in functional connectivity, leveraging a large multi-site dataset of resting-state MRI from 1276 MDD patients and 1104 matched healthy controls. Subject-level extreme functional connections, determined by comparing against normative ranges derived from healthy controls using tolerance intervals, were used to identify biological subtypes of MDD. We identi /uniFB01 ed a set of extreme functional connections that were predominantly between the visual network and the frontoparietal network, the default mode network and the ventral attention network, with the key regions in the anterior cingulate cortex, bilateral orbitofrontal cortex, and supramarginal gyrus. In MDD patients, these extreme functional connections were linked to age of onset and reward-related processes. Using these features, we identi /uniFB01 ed two subtypes with distinct patterns of functional connectivity abnormalities compared to healthy controls ( p &lt; 0.05, Bonferroni correction). When considering all patients together, no signi /uniFB01 cant differences were found. These subtypes signi /uniFB01 cantly enhanced case-control discriminability and showed strong internal discriminability between subtypes. Furthermore, the subtypes were reproducible across varying parameters, study sites, and in untreated patients. Our /uniFB01 ndings provide new insights into the taxonomy and have potential implications for both diagnosis and treatment of MDD.
19:## INTRODUCTION
23:Resting-state functional magnetic resonance imaging (rsfMRI) is a powerful tool for examining altered functional organization in clinical populations [9]. Functional connectivity, measured by correlating spontaneous /uniFB02 uctuations in MRI signals, provides a framework for understanding various aspects of human behavior [10]. Previous rsfMRI studies have shown that MDD is associated with dysfunction and abnormal functional connectivity in frontostriatal and limbic networks [11], consistent with /uniFB01 ndings from chronic stress models in rodents [12 -14]. Patients with different MDD subtypes exhibit distinct patterns of functional connectivity abnormalities, and hence these pro /uniFB01 les have been used to identify novel neurobiological subtypes in neuropsychiatric disorders [3, 7, 15 -18]. However, several challenges remain in identifying neuroimaging-based subtypes of MDD. First, it remains dif /uniFB01 cult to extract features that best capture the neurobiological
31:To address these issues, this study aimed to identify MDD subtypes based on subject-level functional connectivity abnormalities using a tolerance interval-based approach. A tolerance interval is a statistical measure used to estimate the range that contains a speci /uniFB01 ed proportion of a population with a certain con /uniFB01 dence level. This method has been used to infer individual abnormalities relative to a reference population [22, 29, 30]. The neuroimaging data were sourced from the REST-meta-MDD consortium (http://rfmri.org/REST-meta-MDD) [31, 32], which includes rsfMRI scans from 25 research sites with 1276 patients with MDD and 1104 healthy controls. We constructed parcel-wise functional connectivity tolerance intervals using healthy controls and identi /uniFB01 ed subject-level extreme functional connectivity pro /uniFB01 les in MDD patients. These features were used to identify MDD subtypes with distinct patterns of abnormalities. We assessed the reproducibility and sensitivity of these subtypes using subsampling validation, leave-one-site-out validation, different parameter settings, and analysis restricted to untreated patients. Finally, we evaluated whether these subtypes could improve case-control discriminability.
33:## MATERIALS AND METHODS
37:The study was approved by the research ethical committee of the First Af /uniFB01 liated Hospital of Zhengzhou University (2018-KY-88), all methods were performed in accordance with the relevant guidelines and regulations. The imaging datasets for MDD used in this study were sourced from the REST-meta-MDD consortium (http://rfmri.org/RESTmeta-MDD) [31, 32].These data include rsfMRI data from 25 research sites, comprising 1276 MDD patients (mean age: 36.23 ± 21.38 years, 63.71% females) and 1104 HCs (mean age: 36.15 ± 24.55 years, 58.06% females). Ethical approval for data collection was obtained from local research ethics committees at all sites. MDD diagnoses were made by experienced psychiatrists according to DSM-IV criteria, with symptom severity assessed using the Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (HAMD) [33]. The MDD cohort, included 538 /uniFB01 rst-episode individuals, 282 recurrent cases, 447 medication-naive patients and 408 patients receiving treatment (Table 1). Further demographic, clinical and imaging details are available in previous publications [31, 32].
39:Preprocessing of the fMRI data followed standard analysis protocols across study sites to minimize analytical variation. Detailed descriptions of these steps are provided elsewhere [31, 32]. For this study, we utilized the released mean time courses for each brain region, as de /uniFB01 ned in the Craddock 200 brain atlas, provided by the REST-meta-MDD consortium (http://rfmri.org/REST-meta-MDD) [34]. Global signal regression was not applied, given its potential implications for the understanding the pathogenesis of mental disorders [35, 36].
45:Given the multi-site nature of the data collection, differences in scans and acquisition protocols could introduce non-biological variability. To address this, we applied the Combat method to harmonize the functional
47:Table 1. Demographic and clinical information of participants.
70:connections across these sites. Combat has been shown to effectively remove site-related variance while preserving biological variability in neuroimaging studies [37 -39].
74:To identify extreme functional connections in MDD patients relative to HCs, we followed a series of steps. First, we used the ' tolerance ' package in R (https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/tolerance/tolerance.pdf) to estimate nonparametric two-sided tolerance intervals for each functional connection in HCs. Following prior studies, we set a 90% tolerance proportion with a 10% con /uniFB01 dence level [22]. Functional connections in individual subjects (both MDD and HCs) fell outside this range were classi /uniFB01 ed as extreme. Next, enrichment analysis, based on the hypergeometric cumulative distribution function, was then performed to identify connections that were signi /uniFB01 cantly more prevalent in MDD patients compared to HCs [40]. In neuroimaging, enrichment analysis quanti /uniFB01 es the signi /uniFB01 cance of the overlap between connectivity /uniFB01 ndings and previously de /uniFB01 ned functional networks [41, 42]. This process was repeated 100 times, with 90% of the MDD patients randomly subsampled in each iteration. A consistency rate, representing the proportion of repetitions in which a connection was selected, was computed for each connection. Those with a consistency rate greater than 95% were designated as extreme functional connections in MDD patients and categorized into seven canonical resting networks [43]. For comparison, group-level abnormal functional connections were also identi /uniFB01 ed using two-sample t-tests.
80:We also constructed linear regression models to explore the relationship between extreme functional connections and clinical characteristics including symptom severity, illness duration and age of onset were constructed. Signi /uniFB01 cance was set at p &lt; 0.05 with Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons.
84:We next explored whether extreme functional connections could unveil potential MDD subtypes. The signi /uniFB01 cance of clustering was assessed using the SigClust approach, which tests whether the data /uniFB01 t a single Gaussian distribution (i.e., no subtypes) [48]. After con /uniFB01 rming the presence of subtypes, we used the K-means algorithm classify MDD patients based on extreme functional connections, with Euclidean distance as the distance metric. To avoid local minima, K-means was run 100 times with different centroid initializations [49]. The optimal number of subtypes, ranging from 2 -10, was determined using a cluster ensemble voting technique, which considers 26 indices to assess clustering quality [50].
86:Cluster stability was evaluated using 100 randomly selected subsample (90% of patients) and leave-one-site out validation. Co-occurrence rates between patient pairs across iterations were summarized into a single stability index, as detailed in the supplementary methods.
90:We then characterized the identi /uniFB01 ed MDD subtypes using demographic and clinical variables such as sex, age, illness duration, symptom severity, and the proportion of /uniFB01 rst-episode versus recurrent and untreated versus treated patients. These comparisons were conducted using t-tests or chisquare tests, as appropriate. Functional connectivity abnormalities for each subtype, relative to HCs, were also assessed using two-sample t-tests, controlling for sex, age, study site, and head motion (mean frame-wise displacement, mean FD) [51, 52]. Signi /uniFB01 cance was set at p &lt; 0.05 with Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons.
94:Then, we examined whether the identi /uniFB01 ed subtypes were useful and led to an improvement of classi /uniFB01 cation accuracy. We trained linear support vector machines (SVMs) on extreme functional connections to distinguish between patients and HCs. Classi /uniFB01 cation performance was evaluated using a 100 × 10-fold cross-validation framework, yielding a 100 × 1 accuracy vector. Accuracy signi /uniFB01 cance was determined using 100 times random label permutations for each subtype separately. Performance differences between models (models of subtypes vs. models of all patients) were compared using two sample t-tests with signi /uniFB01 cance was set at p &lt; 0.05 after Bonferroni correction.
116:Signi /uniFB01 cant cluster test results showed that there exhibited signi /uniFB01 cant MDD subtypes with extreme functional connections as features (Figure S2). The cluster ensemble voting technique identi /uniFB01 ed the 2-cluster solution as optimal for the entire MDD sample (Fig. 2A). The K-means algorithm classi /uniFB01 ed the patients into two subtypes (subtype 1, N = 394; subtype 2, N = 882). Cluster stability was assessed through 100 subsampling iterations, yielding a stability index of 0.39, signi /uniFB01 cantly higher than chance (permutation p &lt; 0.01). Leave-one-site-out validation yielded a stability index of 0.44 (permutation p &lt; 0.01).
