167:   167	A growing body of research aims to identify subject-level neuroimaging abnormalities, acknowledging the notable heterogeneity in neuroimaging features among patients with MDD. Previous studies have shown that group-level neuroimaging /uniFB01 ndings, which essentially re /uniFB02 ect an ' average patient ' , capture only a subset of patients and often overlook important inter- individual heterogeneity [19, 20]. Several methods have been proposed to investigate individualized abnormalities in structural and functional neuroimaging metrics, such as gray matter morphology and structural covariance [26, 47, 54, 55]. However, studies focusing on abnormal functional connectivity at the subject level, especially in MDD, remains scarce [56 -58]. In this study, we employed a novel method, namely the tolerance interval which does not require prede /uniFB01 ned regions of interest, complex models or preset parameters. This method allowed us to identify subject-level functional connectivity abnormalities in MDD patients. We found that extreme functional connections were concentrated between the visual network and the frontoparietal, DMN and ventral attention network. Key nodes included the ACC, orbitofrontal cortex and supramarginal gyrus. These connections were closely related to cognitive processes including reward, motivation and pain and are associated with age of onset in MDD. The age of onset is consistently recognized as a known source of clinical heterogeneity and a factor that has contributed to con /uniFB02 icting /uniFB01 ndings in previous neuroimaging studies [10, 47, 59, 60]. For comparison, we also used a traditional group-level approach to identify abnormal functional connections, which yielded a distinct spatial distribution from the extreme connections identi /uniFB01 ed using the tolerance interval method. What ' s more, Subtyping based on the tolerance interval method revealed more stable MDD subtypes than those based on group-level features.
